1. WHAT YOU MAY DO

   a. Use the Software for your own gameplay, on machines you own or control.

   b. Read and study the source code.

   c. Modify the source code for your own use, and play with your modified copy.

   d. Keep a copy of the Software, including a modified copy, in a private
      repository or private backup of your own.

   e. Fork the official repository using the hosting platform's own fork
      feature, and keep that fork for as long as the platform allows. Forking is
      how anyone reads, tries and contributes to a public project, and this
      license does not try to take it away. What a fork may not become is a
      separate or alternative distribution of the Software: presented as its own
      project, renamed, advertised as a place to get Lune, or used as a route
      around section 2.

   f. Quote short excerpts of the source code — enough to make a point, not
      enough to rebuild the Software — when discussing it, reviewing it,
      reporting a bug, or asking for or giving help.

   g. Add Lune to a free modpack published on Modrinth by listing it: the
      modpack's entry points at the Author's own Modrinth project, and Modrinth
      delivers the Author's own file to the player. That is the whole permission.
      You are adding Lune to a list, not shipping a copy of it — the file never
      leaves the Author's project page, and the launcher fetches it from there.

      So this does not let you bundle the jar inside the pack, in an overrides
      folder or anywhere else, upload it as a project of your own, or rename,
      repackage or modify it. The modpack must credit Lune, link to the official
      repository, and must not present Lune as someone else's work.

      The Author publishes the Software on a Modrinth project page under the
      Author's control, and Modrinth may host and deliver it for that page and
      for the modpack listings above.


2. WHAT YOU MAY NOT DO

   a. Publish, redistribute, mirror, re-upload or otherwise make the Software
      available to anyone else, in source or compiled form, whole or in part,
      modified or unmodified, except for the Modrinth permissions in section
      1(g). This includes other mod portals, launchers, modpacks, file hosts,
      archives, and any repository other than a platform fork permitted by
      section 1(e).

   b. Sell, license, rent or otherwise commercially exploit the Software, or
      include a copy of it in anything offered for payment, donation or other
      benefit.

   c. Publish a derivative work, port, rewrite or reimplementation that is based
      on the Software or its source code.

   d. Remove, obscure or alter this license, the copyright notice, or the
      attribution shown by the Software.

   Playing with the Software is not redistribution, and neither is publishing
   video or streams of your own gameplay. Gameplay content stays permitted when
   it earns you money — advertising, subscriptions, donations, sponsorship.
   Section 2(b) is about copies of the Software, not about what you record while
   playing with it.


